# ── util: the utility-model workload ────────────────────────────────────────
# What the "utility" role (ling3 today, not the main chat model) actually runs:
# Open WebUI's background tasks -- title, tags, retrieval query, follow-ups -- plus
# n8n's classify/extract nodes. They share one shape that NO other preset has: a
# long prompt (the whole chat history, pasted into a single user message) for a
# ~20-token answer. Prefill dominates and the user is watching a spinner, so the
# row to read is `t`, not t/s. The template below is Open WebUI 0.11's stock title
# task verbatim in shape; the history is a fixed 4-turn ops conversation (~1.8k
# tokens) so that every model gets the same input.
_UTIL_CHAT = '''USER: I have Postgres 16 running in Docker for a few self-hosted apps and right now my only backup is the nightly snapshot of the whole volume. How bad is that?

ASSISTANT: It is better than nothing, but a filesystem snapshot of a live Postgres data directory is a crash-consistent copy, not a clean backup. Restoring it puts the server through crash recovery: it replays WAL from the last checkpoint and usually comes up fine, but "usually" is doing a lot of work in that sentence. If the snapshot is not atomic across the whole data directory -- which it is not if your volume driver copies files one by one, or if pg_wal lives on a different volume -- you can get a torn state that will not start at all, and you will only find out on the day you need it. There is also no way to restore a single database or a single table out of it, and no way to roll back to a point in time between two snapshots.

The usual answer is two layers. The first is a logical dump, pg_dump per database or pg_dumpall for the whole cluster including roles. It is slow to restore for a large database but it is portable, it verifies that the data is actually readable, and it lets you pull one table back. The second is a physical base backup with WAL archiving, pg_basebackup plus archive_command, which is what gives you point-in-time recovery. For a handful of small self-hosted apps the first layer alone is often enough.

USER: Let us start with the dumps then. The containers are managed by Docker Compose, and I would rather not install the Postgres client tools on the host.

ASSISTANT: You do not have to. Run the dump from a throwaway container on the same Compose network, using the same image tag as the server so the client version always matches the server version -- a newer pg_dump can read an older server, but the reverse fails, and having them come from one image tag removes the question entirely.

Something like this as a one-shot service, kept out of the default startup with a profile so a plain compose up does not trigger it:

  backup:
    image: postgres:16
    profiles: [backup]
    environment:
      PGHOST: db
      PGUSER: postgres
      PGPASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/pg_password
    volumes:
      - ./backups:/backups
    entrypoint: /bin/sh
    command: -c "pg_dumpall --clean --if-exists | gzip > /backups/cluster-$$(date +%F).sql.gz"

Then compose run --rm backup from cron or a systemd timer. A few details that matter more than they look. Use --clean --if-exists so the restore is idempotent instead of failing on objects that already exist. Pipe through gzip rather than dumping and compressing afterwards, so you never need twice the disk. Write to a temporary name and rename it into place at the end, because a half-written dump that looks like a finished one is the classic way to discover you have no backups. And check the exit status of pg_dumpall, not of gzip: in a shell pipeline the status you get is the last command in it, which is why set -o pipefail exists.

USER: Good point on pipefail. How do I know the dumps actually restore, without a lot of ceremony?

ASSISTANT: You restore them, on a schedule, automatically. An untested backup is a hypothesis. The cheap version of this is a second one-shot container that starts an empty Postgres, feeds it the newest dump, and then runs a couple of assertions against the result: the restore exited zero, the expected databases exist, a few known tables have a row count above zero, and the newest row in whatever your busiest table is has a timestamp from the last day or two. That last check is the one that catches the interesting failure, where the dump succeeds every night but has been dumping an empty or stale database since somebody changed a user or a search path.

Run it weekly, not nightly, unless the data is small. Keep the output of the last run somewhere you will actually see it -- a file the monitoring stack tails, or a push notification on failure. Silence is not a success signal; if the timer itself stops firing you want to hear about that too, which means alerting on the absence of a recent success rather than on the presence of a failure.

Retention is the other half. Keep something like seven daily, four weekly and six monthly copies, and prune by a script that counts what it is keeping rather than by find -mtime, so that a gap in the dumps cannot silently delete everything. And keep at least one copy off the machine. A backup on the same host protects you from a bad migration, not from a dead disk or a mistyped rm.

USER: Makes sense. Last thing: the apps also have their own upload directories on disk. Should those go in the same job?

ASSISTANT: Keep them in the same schedule but not in the same step. File trees and databases fail differently and restore differently, and you want to be able to run one without the other.

For the files, a snapshotting copy tool beats a tarball: restic or borg against a local repository plus a remote one gives you deduplication, encryption and cheap point-in-time restores, where a nightly tar of an upload directory quickly becomes gigabytes of the same bytes. Both handle the "files changed while I was reading them" case by just recording what they saw, which for user uploads is acceptable, since a newly uploaded file that is missing from tonight will be in tomorrow.

The ordering matters if the database references the files. Back up the files first, then the database. That way the database copy can only reference files that the file copy already has; the other order can leave you with rows pointing at uploads that were never captured. On restore you do the same thing in the same order, and anything uploaded in the window between the two is simply absent, which is a much easier state to reason about than a row that points nowhere.
'''

